One problem with Their Worships' casual development cycle is that some
of these bugs have existed in the released game for several years.
There hasn't even been a bugfix or minor release in 2 1/2 years, yet
the buglist has been slowly populating all that time, up to 194 core
bugs now.  It then falls to 3rd party casual developers to fix some of
these bugs, as well as add gameplay improvements or new features,
which most of the playing public quite rightly doesn't expect to wait
years for.  As a result, these haphazard (though often well-coded)
efforts lack central coordination, work gets duplicated, it doesn't
benefit from standard project management practices, etc.

I've taken flack for saying all this before.  Yes, the devteam is
maintaining the game for free.  Yes, they're generous people for doing
this.  But I believe a better model for nethack development would be
something like sourceforge - viewable and taking coding and other
contributions from the public, active, vibrant, responsible.  The
devteam does have a project there
(https://sourceforge.net/projects/nethack) but either it is not
actively used or there are restrictions on the project to cut off the
public from the underlying activity.

It's a great game.  The people who work on it are great.  But so much
more can be done with it with more transparency and less secrecy and a
little more pep, harnessing the energy of the other enthusiastic
programmers out there in more efficient ways.  Note that it does NOT
follow that the project then descends into chaos - sourceforge-type
projects can still be kept firmly under a central administration.

Bottom line for me is that these multi-year waits are out of
proportion to the bugs and improvements accumulating, and the whole
chaotic ecology of patch and bugfix and add-on websites which spring
up around these waits is crazy.  Then when an official release does
come out, all the 3rd party site maintainers have to independently
update their material to the new official core, itself a process which
can take months or even years.

Centralization and coordination is the answer to this, and a little
bit loosened grip on the "official" imprimatur.  Secrecy and
casualness might have worked well in the 80s or 90s, but less well
today.

Jim


